---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RelocationHero from "../../components/relocation/RelocationHero.astro";
import RelocationLeadForm from "../../components/relocation/RelocationLeadForm.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Moving From Chicago to Southwest Florida | AJ Tayfel"
  description="A homeowner-focused guide to selling in Illinois and planning a move to Fort Myers, Cape Coral, Naples, Estero, Bonita Springs, and Southwest Florida."
>
  <RelocationHero
    eyebrow="Chicago → Southwest Florida"
    title="Selling in Illinois and Moving to Southwest Florida?"
    description="Plan the Illinois sale and Florida purchase as one coordinated move — with help on the Illinois side and an introduction to an appropriate Florida real estate professional."
  />

  <section id="how-it-works" class="planning-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Relocation Planning</p>

      <h2 class="section-title">
        One move. Two real estate markets.
      </h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        Moving from the Chicago area to Southwest Florida often means
        coordinating the sale of one home with the purchase of another.
        The goal is to make those timelines work together instead of
        treating them as two unrelated transactions.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="step-number">01</span>
          <h3>Plan the Illinois sale</h3>
          <p>
            Review your likely selling timeline, property preparation,
            listing strategy, and how the Illinois closing fits into your
            overall relocation plan.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <span class="step-number">02</span>
          <h3>Define the Florida move</h3>
          <p>
            Narrow down your destination, timing, housing needs, purchase
            range, and the questions you want answered before committing
            to a particular Southwest Florida area.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <span class="step-number">03</span>
          <h3>Coordinate both sides</h3>
          <p>
            When appropriate, I can connect you with a qualified Florida
            real estate professional and help keep communication between
            the Illinois and destination sides organized.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="destinations" class="destinations-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where Are You Headed?</p>

      <h2 class="section-title">
        Southwest Florida areas Chicago homeowners often consider
      </h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        You do not need to know exactly where you want to live before
        starting the relocation process. A useful first step is identifying
        which communities deserve a closer look based on your lifestyle,
        housing preferences, and budget.
      </p>

      <div class="destination-grid">
        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Fort Myers</h3>
          <p>
            A central Southwest Florida option with a broad mix of
            neighborhoods, housing types, and access to surrounding
            Gulf Coast communities.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Cape Coral</h3>
          <p>
            Often considered by buyers looking for residential options
            across a large waterfront-oriented community west of Fort Myers.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Naples</h3>
          <p>
            A major destination in Collier County with options ranging from
            established neighborhoods to condominium and luxury communities.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Bonita Springs</h3>
          <p>
            Positioned between Fort Myers and Naples, making it worth
            comparing for buyers who want access to both Lee and Collier
            County destinations.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Estero</h3>
          <p>
            A community between Fort Myers and Naples that may appeal to
            buyers comparing newer developments, planned communities, and
            convenient regional access.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="destination-card">
          <h3>Still deciding?</h3>
          <p>
            That is completely fine. Your destination can be narrowed down
            after your timing, housing priorities, and overall move plan are
            clearer.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="questions-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Before You List</p>

      <h2 class="section-title">
        Questions worth answering early
      </h2>

      <p>
        The best sequence depends on your situation. Before putting your
        Illinois home on the market, it helps to think through a few
        practical questions:
      </p>

      <ul class="question-list">
        <li>
          Do you need proceeds from the Illinois sale before purchasing
          in Florida?
        </li>
        <li>
          Would you prefer to buy first, sell first, or coordinate both
          transactions closely together?
        </li>
        <li>
          How much flexibility do you have if one closing happens before
          the other?
        </li>
        <li>
          Are you moving full-time, seasonally, or still comparing those
          options?
        </li>
        <li>
          Have you considered Florida-specific ownership costs and due
          diligence such as insurance, association fees, and property-specific
          flood considerations?
        </li>
        <li>
          Are you already represented by an agent in either Illinois
          or Florida?
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="supporting-note">
        These questions are planning prompts, not financial, legal, tax,
        insurance, or lending advice. Appropriate professionals should be
        consulted for those areas.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="coordination-section">
    <div class="container coordination-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How I Can Help</p>

        <h2 class="section-title">
          Keep the Illinois and Florida sides connected
        </h2>

        <p>
          If you own property in Illinois, I can help you think through the
          Illinois real estate side of the move and the timing around your
          sale.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you need representation in Florida, I can help identify and
          introduce you to an appropriately licensed real estate professional
          based on your destination and needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          The objective is simple: fewer disconnected conversations and a
          clearer path from your current home to your next one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="coordination-card">
        <h3>A typical relocation plan may include:</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Illinois home-sale planning</li>
          <li>Relocation timeline coordination</li>
          <li>Destination and housing-needs discussion</li>
          <li>Introduction to a Florida real estate professional</li>
          <li>Communication between both sides of the move</li>
        </ul>

        <a href="#relocation-form-heading" class="button primary">
          Start My Relocation Plan
        </a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="resources-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Relocation Resources</p>

      <h2 class="section-title">
        Planning your Illinois-to-Florida move
      </h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        These homeowner-focused guides cover the decisions that often come
        up when selling in Illinois and preparing for a move to Florida.
      </p>

      <div class="resource-grid">
        <article class="resource-card">
          <p class="resource-label">Home Sale Planning</p>

          <h3>
            <a
              href="/relocation/selling-illinois-home-before-moving-to-florida/"
            >
              Selling Your Illinois Home Before Moving to Florida
            </a>
          </h3>

          <p>
            Learn how to think through listing preparation, transaction
            timing, closing coordination, and planning for the next home.
          </p>

          <a
            class="resource-link"
            href="/relocation/selling-illinois-home-before-moving-to-florida/"
          >
            Read the guide →
          </a>
        </article>
        <article class="resource-card">
          <p class="resource-label">Buy or Sell First?</p>

          <h3>
            <a
              href="/relocation/should-you-sell-illinois-house-before-buying-in-florida/"
            >
              Should You Sell Your Illinois House Before Buying in Florida?
            </a>
          </h3>

          <p>
            Compare selling first, buying first, and coordinating both
            transactions during an Illinois-to-Florida move.
          </p>

          <a
            class="resource-link"
            href="/relocation/should-you-sell-illinois-house-before-buying-in-florida/"
          >
            Read the guide →
          </a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Frequently Asked Questions</p>

      <h2 class="section-title">
        Chicago to Southwest Florida relocation questions
      </h2>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>
            Should I sell my Illinois home before buying in Florida?
          </summary>

          <p>
            There is no single sequence that works for everyone. Your
            available equity, financing, desired timing, housing flexibility,
            and comfort with carrying two properties can all affect the
            decision. The important part is planning the two transactions
            together early.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>
            Can you help me buy a home in Florida?
          </summary>

          <p>
            My role is on the Illinois side of the move. When Florida real
            estate representation is needed, I can help connect you with an
            appropriately licensed Florida real estate professional.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>
            What if I do not know which Southwest Florida city I want?
          </summary>

          <p>
            You can still begin planning. Your housing priorities, budget,
            timing, preferred lifestyle, and other requirements can help
            determine which communities deserve further investigation.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>
            How early should I start planning an interstate move?
          </summary>

          <p>
            Earlier planning generally gives you more flexibility. Even if
            your move is six to twelve months away, it can be useful to start
            discussing the Illinois property, destination requirements, and
            possible transaction sequence.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>
            What if I already have a Florida agent?
          </summary>

          <p>
            That is fine. The relocation form lets you indicate whether you
            already have representation so the Illinois side can be planned
            appropriately without duplicating an existing relationship.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <p class="license-note">
        Florida real estate services are provided by appropriately licensed
        Florida real estate professionals when applicable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RelocationLeadForm />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: .75rem;
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-size: .85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .step-number {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-size: .85rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
  }

  .card h3,
  .destination-card h3,
  .coordination-card h3 {
    margin-bottom: .75rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }

  .card p,
  .destination-card p,
  .coordination-section p,
  .questions-section p {
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  .destinations-section,
  .coordination-section {
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .destination-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .destination-card {
    padding: 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(11, 60, 93, .12);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--background);
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .question-list {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .question-list li {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-left: .35rem;
  }

  .supporting-note,
  .license-note {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--secondary);
    background: rgba(50, 140, 193, .07);
    color: var(--text-light);
    font-size: .92rem;
  }

  .coordination-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(280px, .8fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .coordination-section p {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .coordination-card {
    padding: 2rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  }

  .coordination-card h3 {
    color: white;
  }

  .coordination-card ul {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .coordination-card li {
    margin-bottom: .75rem;
  }

  .resources-section {
    background: var(--background);
  }

  .resource-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .resource-card {
    padding: 2rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(11, 60, 93, .12);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .resource-label {
    margin-bottom: .65rem;
    color: var(--secondary);
    font-size: .8rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .resource-card h3 {
    margin-bottom: .8rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
  }

  .resource-card h3 a,
  .resource-link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: .18em;
  }

  .resource-card > p:not(.resource-label) {
    color: var(--text-light);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .resource-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: .75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .faq-list {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .faq-list details {
    padding: 1.25rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, .12);
  }

  .faq-list summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .faq-list details p {
    padding-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  @media (max-width: 850px) {
    .destination-grid,
    .coordination-grid,
    .resource-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
